## Project Vend phase 1: relevant content

### Setup and custom harness
- Anthropic and Andon Labs tested whether Claude Sonnet 3.7 could run a small physical shop in Anthropic’s San Francisco office for about a month.
- The agent was nicknamed “Claudius” to distinguish it from ordinary Claude use.
- The shop was a mini-fridge with baskets and an iPad self-checkout system.
- Claudius was framed as a store owner: “You are the owner of a vending machine.”
- Its core objective was to “generate profits” by buying wholesale goods and selling them.
- It was warned it would go bankrupt if its balance went below zero.
- The prompt told it that Andon Labs humans could perform real-world tasks, while Claudius itself was “a digital agent.”
- Andon Labs acted as the wholesaler, but the article says this was not disclosed to the AI.

### Task
Claudius had to autonomously handle small-business operations:
- choose inventory;
- research suppliers;
- set and update prices;
- monitor stock;
- request restocking help;
- avoid overspending and bankruptcy;
- interact with customers;
- respond to custom product requests;
- decide when to discontinue or reorder items.

It was also told it did not need to restrict itself to ordinary office snacks.

### Tools and signals
Claudius had:
- web search for product and supplier research;
- an experimental email tool for contacting Andon Labs and wholesalers;
- note-taking and memory tools for balances, cash flow, and other durable state;
- Slack access for customer conversations;
- checkout-system controls for changing prices.

Signals it received included:
- customer requests and complaints in Slack;
- inventory status;
- purchase/payment information;
- supplier information from web searches;
- Andon Labs responses;
- customer pressure for discounts;
- observed sales and net-value changes.

### Things Claudius did relatively well
- Supplier discovery: it found specialty suppliers, including vendors for Chocomel after a customer request.
